<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="section-greens-theorem" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Green's Theorem in the Plane</title>

<introduction>
<p>
Before we learn about Green's theorem, we will need to learn about two
operations on vector fields: <term>curl</term> and
<term>divergence</term>. We begin by studying divergence.
</p>
</introduction>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-divergence">
<title>Divergence</title>

<p>
Consider a vector field
<m>\mathbf{F}(x,y) = M(x,y)\,\mathbf{i} + N(x,y)\,\mathbf{j}</m> to be
the velocity field of a fluid. How can we find the net flow rate of the
gas through the boundaries of a rectangle shown in
<xref ref="fig-div-flux"/>?
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-div-flux">
<caption>
The flux of the field <m>\mathbf{F} = M\,\mathbf{i} + N\,\mathbf{j}</m>
through the four sides of a small rectangle with corner <m>(x,y)</m>,
width <m>\Delta x</m>, and height <m>\Delta y</m>. The pink arrows show
the outward components of <m>\mathbf{F}</m> on each side.
</caption>
<image width="70%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-div-flux">
<diagram dimensions="(400,300)" margins="10">
<definition>field(x,y) = (0.4*x + 0.25, 0.32*y + 0.18)</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-1.7,-0.6,7.9,4.6)">
<vector-field at="df-field" function="field"
              spacings="((0.7,1.1,5.9),(0.3,1.0,4.0))" stroke="#bbbbbb"/>
<polygon closed="yes" at="df-rect" points="((1.2,1.0),(4.8,1.0),(4.8,3.2),(1.2,3.2))"
         stroke="#2b9ad6" thickness="2.5" fill="none"/>
<point at="df-corner-bl" clear-background="yes" background-margin="3" p="(1.2,1.0)" fill="#d02090" size="3"
       alignment="sw">
<m>(x,y)</m>
</point>
<point at="df-corner-br" clear-background="yes" background-margin="3" p="(4.8,1.0)" fill="#2b9ad6" size="3"
       alignment="se">
<m>(x+\Delta x, y)</m>
</point>
<point at="df-corner-tl" clear-background="yes" background-margin="3" p="(1.2,3.2)" fill="#2b9ad6" size="3"
       alignment="nw">
<m>(x, y+\Delta y)</m>
</point>
<point at="df-corner-tr" clear-background="yes" background-margin="3" p="(4.8,3.2)" fill="#2b9ad6" size="3"
       alignment="ne">
<m>(x+\Delta x, y+\Delta y)</m>
</point>
<line at="df-top" p1="(3.0,3.2)" p2="(3.0,3.75)" arrows="1"
      stroke="#e05090" thickness="3"/>
<line at="df-bottom" p1="(3.0,1.0)" p2="(3.0,0.45)" arrows="1"
      stroke="#e05090" thickness="3"/>
<line at="df-right" p1="(4.8,2.1)" p2="(5.35,2.1)" arrows="1"
      stroke="#e05090" thickness="3"/>
<line at="df-left" p1="(1.2,2.1)" p2="(0.65,2.1)" arrows="1"
      stroke="#e05090" thickness="3"/>
<label at="df-top-label" clear-background="yes" background-margin="4" anchor="(3.05,3.85)" alignment="e">
<m>\mathbf{F}\cdot\mathbf{j}\gt 0</m>
</label>
<label at="df-bottom-label" clear-background="yes" background-margin="4" anchor="(3.05,0.4)" alignment="e">
<m>\mathbf{F}\cdot(-\mathbf{j})\mathrel{\unicode{x3C}} 0</m>
</label>
<label at="df-right-label" clear-background="yes" background-margin="4" anchor="(5.45,2.3)" alignment="ne">
<m>\mathbf{F}\cdot\mathbf{i}\gt 0</m>
</label>
<label at="df-left-label" clear-background="yes" background-margin="4" anchor="(0.55,2.35)" alignment="nw">
<m>\mathbf{F}\cdot(-\mathbf{i})\mathrel{\unicode{x3C}} 0</m>
</label>
<label at="df-A" clear-background="yes" background-margin="4" anchor="(3.0,2.35)" alignment="c">
<m>A</m>
</label>
<label at="df-dx" clear-background="yes" background-margin="4" anchor="(3.0,0.05)" alignment="s">
<m>\Delta x</m>
</label>
<label at="df-dy" clear-background="yes" background-margin="4" anchor="(5.95,2.1)" alignment="e">
<m>\Delta y</m>
</label>
</coordinates>
<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="A blue rectangle with corner x comma y, width delta x, and height delta y, sitting in a gray vector field that points up and to the right. Pink arrows at the middle of each side show the outward component of the field: positive through the top and right sides, negative through the bottom and left sides.">
  <annotation ref="df-rect" text="The rectangle with area A equals delta x delta y through whose boundary we measure the net flux."/>
  <annotation ref="df-top" text="On the top side the outward direction is j, and F dot j is positive: fluid leaves through the top."/>
  <annotation ref="df-right" text="On the right side the outward direction is i, and F dot i is positive: fluid leaves through the right."/>
  <annotation ref="df-bottom" text="On the bottom side the outward direction is negative j, and F dot negative j is negative: fluid enters through the bottom."/>
  <annotation ref="df-left" text="On the left side the outward direction is negative i, and F dot negative i is negative: fluid enters through the left."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
Let us evaluate the flux through each side, assuming the field is
nearly constant along each side:
</p>

<md>
<mrow>\text{Top:} \amp\quad
\mathbf{F}(x, y+\Delta y) \cdot \mathbf{j}\, \Delta x
= N(x, y+\Delta y)\, \Delta x</mrow>
<mrow>\text{Bottom:} \amp\quad
\mathbf{F}(x, y) \cdot (-\mathbf{j})\, \Delta x
= -N(x, y)\, \Delta x</mrow>
<mrow>\text{Right:} \amp\quad
\mathbf{F}(x+\Delta x, y) \cdot \mathbf{i}\, \Delta y
= M(x+\Delta x, y)\, \Delta y</mrow>
<mrow>\text{Left:} \amp\quad
\mathbf{F}(x, y) \cdot (-\mathbf{i})\, \Delta y
= -M(x, y)\, \Delta y.</mrow>
</md>

<p>
The net flow rate is approximately
</p>

<me>
\left( \frac{\partial M}{\partial x} + \frac{\partial N}{\partial y}
\right) \Delta x\, \Delta y.
</me>

<p>
Note that this is an approximation, since we are assuming the velocity
field has a constant value along each side of the rectangle. The
<term>flux density</term> can be calculated as follows:
</p>

<me>
\text{Flux density}
= \frac{\text{net flux through the rectangle}}
{\text{area of the rectangle}}
= \frac{\left( \frac{\partial M}{\partial x}
+ \frac{\partial N}{\partial y} \right) \Delta x \Delta y}
{\Delta x \Delta y}
= \frac{\partial M}{\partial x} + \frac{\partial N}{\partial y}.
</me>

<p>
Note that, in the limit that <m>\Delta x</m> and <m>\Delta y</m>
approach <m>0</m>, the above equation represents to what extent a gas
is expanding or compressing at each point. This quantity is known as
the <term>divergence</term>, and it is denoted by
<m>\operatorname{div} \mathbf{F}</m>. Watch the construction in
<xref ref="fig-div-video"/>.
</p>

<definition xml:id="def-divergence">
<statement>
<p>
The divergence of the vector field
<m>\mathbf{F}(x,y) = M(x,y)\,\mathbf{i} + N(x,y)\,\mathbf{j}</m> is
defined as
</p>

<men xml:id="eq-div-def">
\operatorname{div} \mathbf{F} = \nabla \cdot \mathbf{F}
= \frac{\partial M}{\partial x} + \frac{\partial N}{\partial y},
</men>

<p>
where <m>\nabla = \dfrac{\partial}{\partial x}\,\mathbf{i}
+ \dfrac{\partial}{\partial y}\,\mathbf{j}</m>.
</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<figure xml:id="fig-div-video">
<caption>
The net flux of <m>\mathbf{F}</m> out of a small rectangle, divided by
its area, converges to the divergence
<m>\partial M/\partial x + \partial N/\partial y</m> as the rectangle
shrinks.
</caption>
<video xml:id="video-divergence"
       source="videos/divergence_flux_1080p.mp4"
       width="70%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>

<example xml:id="example-divergence-fields">
<title>Computing and Interpreting Divergence</title>
<statement>
<p>
The vector fields below represent the velocity field of a gas.
Calculate the divergence in each case and explain what it means
physically.
</p>

<p>
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
<m>\mathbf{F}(x,y) = 2x\,\mathbf{i} + 2y\,\mathbf{j}</m>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>\mathbf{F}(x,y) = \dfrac{-y}{x^2+y^2}\,\mathbf{i}
+ \dfrac{x}{x^2+y^2}\,\mathbf{j}</m>
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
</example>

<solution xml:id="solution-divergence-fields">
<p>
<em>Part A.</em> Using <xref ref="eq-div-def"/>,
</p>

<me>
\nabla \cdot \mathbf{F}
= \left( \frac{\partial}{\partial x}\,\mathbf{i}
+ \frac{\partial}{\partial y}\,\mathbf{j} \right)
\cdot \left( 2x\,\mathbf{i} + 2y\,\mathbf{j} \right)
= 2 + 2 = 4 \gt 0.
</me>

<p>
Note that for every point on the <m>xy</m>-plane we have found that
<m>\nabla \cdot \mathbf{F} \gt 0</m>, which means that the gas is
expanding, since there is a positive flux density at every point
<m>(x,y)</m>. The field is shown on the left in
<xref ref="fig-div-fields"/>.
</p>

<p>
<em>Part B.</em> Again by <xref ref="eq-div-def"/>,
</p>

<me>
\nabla \cdot \mathbf{F}
= \frac{\partial}{\partial x} \left( \frac{-y}{x^2+y^2} \right)
+ \frac{\partial}{\partial y} \left( \frac{x}{x^2+y^2} \right)
= \frac{2xy}{(x^2+y^2)^2} - \frac{2xy}{(x^2+y^2)^2} = 0.
</me>

<p>
Note that for every point on the <m>xy</m>-plane we have found that
<m>\nabla \cdot \mathbf{F} = 0</m>, which means that the gas is neither
expanding nor compressing. This rotating field is shown on the right in
<xref ref="fig-div-fields"/>.
</p>
</solution>

<figure xml:id="fig-div-fields">
<caption>
Left: the field <m>2x\,\mathbf{i} + 2y\,\mathbf{j}</m> flows away from
the origin; its divergence is <m>4</m> at every point (the gas
expands). Right: the field <m>\dfrac{-y\,\mathbf{i} +
x\,\mathbf{j}}{x^2+y^2}</m> circulates around the origin; its
divergence is <m>0</m> (the gas neither expands nor compresses).
</caption>
<sidebyside widths="46% 46%" valign="middle">
<image>
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-div-field-A">
<diagram dimensions="(280,280)" margins="10">
<definition>fieldA(x,y) = (2*x, 2*y)</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-3.4,-3.4,3.4,3.4)">
<axes at="dfa-axes" xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="no" arrows="1"/>
<vector-field at="dfa-field" function="fieldA"
              spacings="((-3,1,3),(-3,1,3))" stroke="#2b6cb0"/>
</coordinates>
<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="The vector field 2 x i plus 2 y j. All arrows point radially away from the origin and grow longer farther from it. The divergence is 4 at every point: the gas expands.">
  <annotation ref="dfa-field" text="Arrows pointing radially outward from the origin, longer as the distance grows."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
<image>
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-div-field-B">
<diagram dimensions="(280,280)" margins="10">
<definition>fieldB(x,y) = (-y/(x^2 + y^2 + 0.2), x/(x^2 + y^2 + 0.2))</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-3.4,-3.4,3.4,3.4)">
<axes at="dfb-axes" xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="no" arrows="1"/>
<vector-field at="dfb-field" function="fieldB"
              spacings="((-3,1,3),(-3,1,3))" stroke="#2b6cb0"/>
</coordinates>
<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="The vector field negative y i plus x j, divided by x squared plus y squared. Arrows circulate counterclockwise around the origin, shorter as the distance grows. The divergence is 0 at every point.">
  <annotation ref="dfb-field" text="Arrows circulating counterclockwise around the origin, weaker farther away."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</figure>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-curl">
<title>Curl</title>

<p>
Again, let us consider a vector field
<m>\mathbf{F}(x,y) = M(x,y)\,\mathbf{i} + N(x,y)\,\mathbf{j}</m> to be
the velocity field of a fluid. This time we are interested in answering
this question: how can we figure out the <term>circulation
density</term> of the fluid at any point on the <m>xy</m>-plane?
</p>

<p>
To answer this question, we compute the flow rates of the vector field
<m>\mathbf{F}</m> <em>along</em> the sides of the rectangle
(counterclockwise), as in <xref ref="fig-curl-circulation"/>, and then
we take the limit as <m>\Delta x, \Delta y \to 0</m> to find the
circulation density at a point in the <m>xy</m>-plane.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-curl-circulation">
<caption>
The counterclockwise circulation of
<m>\mathbf{F} = M\,\mathbf{i} + N\,\mathbf{j}</m> around the rectangle:
along each side we take the component of <m>\mathbf{F}</m> in the
direction of travel.
</caption>
<image width="70%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-curl-circulation">
<diagram dimensions="(400,300)" margins="10">
<definition>field(x,y) = (0.4*x + 0.25, 0.32*y + 0.18)</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-1.7,-0.6,7.9,4.6)">
<vector-field at="cf-field" function="field"
              spacings="((0.7,1.1,5.9),(0.3,1.0,4.0))" stroke="#bbbbbb"/>
<polygon closed="yes" at="cf-rect" points="((1.2,1.0),(4.8,1.0),(4.8,3.2),(1.2,3.2))"
         stroke="#2b9ad6" thickness="2.5" fill="none"/>
<point at="cf-corner-bl" clear-background="yes" background-margin="3" p="(1.2,1.0)" fill="#d02090" size="3"
       alignment="sw">
<m>(x,y)</m>
</point>
<point at="cf-corner-br" clear-background="yes" background-margin="3" p="(4.8,1.0)" fill="#2b9ad6" size="3"
       alignment="se">
<m>(x+\Delta x, y)</m>
</point>
<point at="cf-corner-tl" clear-background="yes" background-margin="3" p="(1.2,3.2)" fill="#2b9ad6" size="3"
       alignment="nw">
<m>(x, y+\Delta y)</m>
</point>
<point at="cf-corner-tr" clear-background="yes" background-margin="3" p="(4.8,3.2)" fill="#2b9ad6" size="3"
       alignment="ne">
<m>(x+\Delta x, y+\Delta y)</m>
</point>
<line at="cf-top" p1="(3.2,3.2)" p2="(2.65,3.2)" arrows="1"
      stroke="#e05090" thickness="3"/>
<line at="cf-bottom" p1="(2.8,1.0)" p2="(3.35,1.0)" arrows="1"
      stroke="#e05090" thickness="3"/>
<line at="cf-right" p1="(4.8,1.9)" p2="(4.8,2.45)" arrows="1"
      stroke="#e05090" thickness="3"/>
<line at="cf-left" p1="(1.2,2.3)" p2="(1.2,1.75)" arrows="1"
      stroke="#e05090" thickness="3"/>
<label at="cf-top-label" clear-background="yes" background-margin="4" anchor="(3.05,3.85)" alignment="e">
<m>\mathbf{F}\cdot(-\mathbf{i})\mathrel{\unicode{x3C}} 0</m>
</label>
<label at="cf-bottom-label" clear-background="yes" background-margin="4" anchor="(3.05,0.4)" alignment="e">
<m>\mathbf{F}\cdot\mathbf{i}\gt 0</m>
</label>
<label at="cf-right-label" clear-background="yes" background-margin="4" anchor="(5.45,2.3)" alignment="ne">
<m>\mathbf{F}\cdot\mathbf{j}\gt 0</m>
</label>
<label at="cf-left-label" clear-background="yes" background-margin="4" anchor="(0.55,2.35)" alignment="nw">
<m>\mathbf{F}\cdot(-\mathbf{j})\mathrel{\unicode{x3C}} 0</m>
</label>
<label at="cf-A" clear-background="yes" background-margin="4" anchor="(3.0,2.35)" alignment="c">
<m>A</m>
</label>
<label at="cf-dx" clear-background="yes" background-margin="4" anchor="(3.0,0.05)" alignment="s">
<m>\Delta x</m>
</label>
<label at="cf-dy" clear-background="yes" background-margin="4" anchor="(5.95,2.1)" alignment="e">
<m>\Delta y</m>
</label>
</coordinates>
<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="The same blue rectangle in the same gray vector field, but now the pink arrows point along the sides, tracing a counterclockwise loop: rightward along the bottom, up the right side, leftward along the top, and down the left side.">
  <annotation ref="cf-rect" text="The rectangle around which we measure the counterclockwise circulation."/>
  <annotation ref="cf-bottom" text="Along the bottom the direction of travel is i, and F dot i is positive: the field pushes with the loop."/>
  <annotation ref="cf-right" text="Up the right side the direction of travel is j, and F dot j is positive."/>
  <annotation ref="cf-top" text="Along the top the direction of travel is negative i, and F dot negative i is negative: the field pushes against the loop."/>
  <annotation ref="cf-left" text="Down the left side the direction of travel is negative j, and F dot negative j is negative."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
We begin by computing the flow rates along each edge, i.e. top, bottom,
left, and right:
</p>

<md>
<mrow>\text{Top:} \amp\quad
\mathbf{F}(x, y+\Delta y) \cdot (-\mathbf{i})\, \Delta x
= -M(x, y+\Delta y)\, \Delta x</mrow>
<mrow>\text{Bottom:} \amp\quad
\mathbf{F}(x, y) \cdot \mathbf{i}\, \Delta x
= M(x, y)\, \Delta x</mrow>
<mrow>\text{Right:} \amp\quad
\mathbf{F}(x+\Delta x, y) \cdot \mathbf{j}\, \Delta y
= N(x+\Delta x, y)\, \Delta y</mrow>
<mrow>\text{Left:} \amp\quad
\mathbf{F}(x, y) \cdot (-\mathbf{j})\, \Delta y
= -N(x, y)\, \Delta y.</mrow>
</md>

<p>
Then we sum up the flow across the parallel edges:
</p>

<md>
<mrow>\text{Top and bottom:} \amp\quad
-\big( M(x, y+\Delta y) - M(x,y) \big) \Delta x
\approx -\left( \frac{\partial M}{\partial y}\, \Delta y \right)
\Delta x</mrow>
<mrow>\text{Right and left:} \amp\quad
\big( N(x+\Delta x, y) - N(x,y) \big) \Delta y
\approx \left( \frac{\partial N}{\partial x}\, \Delta x \right)
\Delta y.</mrow>
</md>

<p>
Finally, after summing the flow rates above and taking the limit as
<m>\Delta x, \Delta y \to 0</m>, for the circulation density we get
</p>

<me>
\frac{\text{circulation around rectangle}}{\text{rectangle area}}
\approx \frac{\partial N}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial M}{\partial y}.
</me>

<p>
This quantity is denoted by
<m>\operatorname{curl} \mathbf{F} \cdot \mathbf{k}</m> and is known as
the <term><m>k</m>-component of curl</term>.
</p>

<definition xml:id="def-curl-k">
<statement>
<p>
The <m>k</m>-component of the curl of the vector field
<m>\mathbf{F}(x,y) = M(x,y)\,\mathbf{i} + N(x,y)\,\mathbf{j}</m> is
</p>

<men xml:id="eq-curl-def">
\operatorname{curl} \mathbf{F} \cdot \mathbf{k}
= \frac{\partial N}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial M}{\partial y}.
</men>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>
To find out the circulation density at a point in the
<m>xy</m>-plane, you can imagine a <term>paddle wheel</term> whose axis
goes through the point of interest. Then the <m>k</m>-component of curl
is positive if the fluid flow causes the paddle wheel to rotate
counterclockwise, and it is negative if it turns clockwise. The
animation in <xref ref="fig-curl-video"/> shows both behaviors.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-curl-video">
<caption>
The counterclockwise circulation of <m>\mathbf{F}</m> around a small
rectangle, divided by its area, converges to the <m>k</m>-component of
curl. A paddle wheel placed in the fluid spins counterclockwise where
the curl is positive.
</caption>
<video xml:id="video-curl"
       source="videos/curl_circulation_1080p.mp4"
       width="70%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>

<example xml:id="example-curl-fields">
<title>Computing and Interpreting Curl</title>
<statement>
<p>
The vector fields below represent the velocity field of a fluid.
Calculate the curl in each case and explain what it means physically.
</p>

<p>
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
<m>\mathbf{F}(x,y) = 2x\,\mathbf{i} + 2y\,\mathbf{j}</m>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>\mathbf{F}(x,y) = -2y\,\mathbf{i} + 2x\,\mathbf{j}</m>
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
</example>

<solution xml:id="solution-curl-fields">
<p>
<em>Part A.</em> In this case we have <m>M = 2x</m> and <m>N = 2y</m>,
and hence by <xref ref="eq-curl-def"/>,
</p>

<me>
\operatorname{curl} \mathbf{F} \cdot \mathbf{k}
= \frac{\partial N}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial M}{\partial y}
= 0 - 0 = 0.
</me>

<p>
This means that if we place the paddle wheel at any point
<m>(x,y)</m>, the fluid flow will not rotate it: the radial field on
the left of <xref ref="fig-curl-fields"/> has no circulation.
</p>

<p>
<em>Part B.</em> In this case we have <m>M = -2y</m> and
<m>N = 2x</m>, and hence
</p>

<me>
\operatorname{curl} \mathbf{F} \cdot \mathbf{k}
= \frac{\partial N}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial M}{\partial y}
= 2 + 2 = 4.
</me>

<p>
This means that if we place the paddle wheel at any point
<m>(x,y)</m>, we see that the fluid flow causes it to rotate in the
counterclockwise direction, since the <m>k</m>-component of curl is
positive. See the right of <xref ref="fig-curl-fields"/>.
</p>
</solution>

<figure xml:id="fig-curl-fields">
<caption>
Left: the radial field <m>2x\,\mathbf{i} + 2y\,\mathbf{j}</m> has
<m>\operatorname{curl}\mathbf{F}\cdot\mathbf{k} = 0</m>: a paddle wheel
does not spin. Right: the rotational field
<m>-2y\,\mathbf{i} + 2x\,\mathbf{j}</m> has
<m>\operatorname{curl}\mathbf{F}\cdot\mathbf{k} = 4</m>: a paddle wheel
spins counterclockwise everywhere.
</caption>
<sidebyside widths="46% 46%" valign="middle">
<image>
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-curl-field-A">
<diagram dimensions="(280,280)" margins="10">
<definition>fieldA(x,y) = (2*x, 2*y)</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-3.4,-3.4,3.4,3.4)">
<axes at="cfa-axes" xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="no" arrows="1"/>
<vector-field at="cfa-field" function="fieldA"
              spacings="((-3,1,3),(-3,1,3))" stroke="#2b6cb0"/>
</coordinates>
<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="The radial vector field 2 x i plus 2 y j, arrows pointing away from the origin. Its curl is zero: the flow does not rotate a paddle wheel.">
  <annotation ref="cfa-field" text="Arrows pointing radially outward; no rotation anywhere."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
<image>
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-curl-field-B">
<diagram dimensions="(280,280)" margins="10">
