</prefigure>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</figure>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-greens-theorem-forms">
<title>The Two Forms of Green's Theorem</title>

<p>
Next we state Green's theorem in 2D<fn>Note that we only cover Green's
theorem in 2D; however, you should know that this theorem can be
generalized to 3D, and in that case it is known as Stokes' theorem,
which is <xref ref="section-stokes-theorem"/>.</fn>, in two forms. The first form relates the outward flux
across a simple<fn>A curve in the <m>xy</m>-plane is
<term>simple</term> if it does not cross itself.</fn> closed curve
<m>C</m> and the double integral of the divergence over the region
<m>R</m> bounded by the curve <m>C</m>.
</p>

<theorem xml:id="thm-green-normal">
<title>Green's Theorem (Flux-Divergence or Normal Form)</title>
<statement>
<p>
Let <m>C</m> be a piecewise smooth, simple closed curve enclosing a
region <m>R</m> in the plane. Let
<m>\mathbf{F} = M\,\mathbf{i} + N\,\mathbf{j}</m> be a vector field
with <m>M</m> and <m>N</m> having continuous first partial derivatives
in an open region containing <m>R</m>. Then the outward flux of
<m>\mathbf{F}</m> across <m>C</m> equals the double integral of
<m>\operatorname{div} \mathbf{F}</m> over the region <m>R</m> enclosed
by <m>C</m>:
</p>

<men xml:id="eq-green-normal">
\underbrace{\oint_C \mathbf{F} \cdot \mathbf{n}\, ds
= \oint_C M\, dy - N\, dx}_{\text{outward flux through the boundary}}
= \underbrace{\iint_R \left( \frac{\partial M}{\partial x}
+ \frac{\partial N}{\partial y} \right) dx\, dy}_{\text{double integral
of divergence over } R}.
</men>
</statement>
</theorem>

<p>
The second form of Green's theorem connects the counterclockwise
circulation of a vector field around a simple closed curve <m>C</m>
and the double integral of the <m>\mathbf{k}</m>-component of the curl
of the field over the region <m>R</m> enclosed by the curve.
</p>

<theorem xml:id="thm-green-tangential">
<title>Green's Theorem (Circulation-Curl or Tangential Form)</title>
<statement>
<p>
Let <m>C</m> be a piecewise smooth, simple closed curve enclosing a
region <m>R</m> in the plane. Let
<m>\mathbf{F} = M\,\mathbf{i} + N\,\mathbf{j}</m> be a vector field
with <m>M</m> and <m>N</m> having continuous first partial derivatives
in an open region containing <m>R</m>. Then the counterclockwise
circulation of <m>\mathbf{F}</m> around <m>C</m> equals the double
integral of <m>(\operatorname{curl} \mathbf{F}) \cdot \mathbf{k}</m>
over <m>R</m>:
</p>

<men xml:id="eq-green-tangential">
\underbrace{\oint_C \mathbf{F} \cdot \mathbf{T}\, ds
= \oint_C M\, dx + N\, dy}_{\text{counterclockwise circulation}}
= \underbrace{\iint_R \left( \frac{\partial N}{\partial x}
- \frac{\partial M}{\partial y} \right) dx\, dy}_{\text{double integral
of curl} \,\cdot\, \mathbf{k} \text{ over } R}.
</men>
</statement>
</theorem>

<example xml:id="example-green-circle">
<title>Verifying Both Forms on the Unit Circle</title>
<statement>
<p>
Assume that the vector field
<m>\mathbf{F}(x,y) = (x - y)\,\mathbf{i} + x\,\mathbf{j}</m> and the
closed curve <m>C</m> is the unit circle centered at the origin.
Convince yourself of the two forms of Green's theorem by applying them
to this example.
</p>
</statement>
</example>

<solution xml:id="solution-green-circle">
<p>
We begin by noticing that the unit circle can be parametrized as
</p>

<me>
C: \quad \mathbf{r}(t) = (\cos t)\,\mathbf{i} + (\sin t)\,\mathbf{j},
\qquad 0 \le t \le 2\pi.
</me>

<p>
After substituting the parametrized equations <m>x(t) = \cos t</m> and
<m>y(t) = \sin t</m> into the vector field, we get
<m>\mathbf{F} = (\cos t - \sin t)\,\mathbf{i} + (\cos t)\,\mathbf{j}</m>,
and thus
</p>

<md>
<mrow>M = \cos t - \sin t, \amp\qquad dx = d(\cos t) = -\sin t\, dt,</mrow>
<mrow>N = \cos t, \amp\qquad dy = d(\sin t) = \cos t\, dt,</mrow>
</md>

<p>
with the partial derivatives
</p>

<me>
\frac{\partial M}{\partial x} = 1, \qquad
\frac{\partial M}{\partial y} = -1, \qquad
\frac{\partial N}{\partial x} = 1, \qquad
\frac{\partial N}{\partial y} = 0.
</me>

<p>
For the first form of Green's theorem,
<xref ref="eq-green-normal"/>, we have
</p>

<md>
<mrow>\oint_C M\, dy - N\, dx
\amp= \int_0^{2\pi} (\cos t - \sin t)(\cos t\, dt)
- (\cos t)(-\sin t\, dt)</mrow>
<mrow>\amp= \int_0^{2\pi} \cos^2 t\, dt = \pi,</mrow>
</md>

<p>
while the double integral gives the same value:
</p>

<me>
\iint_R \left( \frac{\partial M}{\partial x}
+ \frac{\partial N}{\partial y} \right) dx\, dy
= \iint_R (1 + 0)\, dx\, dy
= \text{area inside the unit circle} = \pi.
</me>

<p>
Whereas the second form of Green's theorem,
<xref ref="eq-green-tangential"/>, can be verified as follows:
</p>

<md>
<mrow>\oint_C M\, dx + N\, dy
\amp= \int_0^{2\pi} (\cos t - \sin t)(-\sin t\, dt)
+ (\cos t)(\cos t\, dt)</mrow>
<mrow>\amp= \int_0^{2\pi} (-\sin t \cos t + 1)\, dt = 2\pi,</mrow>
</md>

<p>
and
</p>

<me>
\iint_R \left( \frac{\partial N}{\partial x}
- \frac{\partial M}{\partial y} \right) dx\, dy
= \iint_R \big( 1 - (-1) \big)\, dx\, dy
= 2 \iint_R dx\, dy = 2\pi.
</me>

<p>
The field and the curve are shown in <xref ref="fig-green-circle"/>;
note that the field has a positive (counterclockwise) circulation,
which we calculated above to be <m>2\pi</m>. Watch both computations
side by side in <xref ref="fig-green-circle-video"/>.
</p>
</solution>

<figure xml:id="fig-green-circle-video">
<caption>
Both forms of Green's theorem verified for
<m>\mathbf{F} = (x-y)\,\mathbf{i} + x\,\mathbf{j}</m> on the unit
circle: the outward flux equals <m>\pi</m> and the counterclockwise
circulation equals <m>2\pi</m>.
</caption>
<video xml:id="video-green-circle"
       source="videos/green_unit_circle_1080p.mp4"
       width="70%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>

<figure xml:id="fig-green-circle">
<caption>
The vector field <m>\mathbf{F}(x,y) = (x-y)\,\mathbf{i} +
x\,\mathbf{j}</m> and the unit circle <m>C</m>. The field has a
positive (counterclockwise) circulation around <m>C</m>.
</caption>
<image width="55%">
