<example xml:id="example-green-diamond">
<title>A Line Integral via Green's Theorem and Substitution</title>
<statement>
<p>
Evaluate the line integral
</p>

<me>
\oint_C \frac{dx}{x+y} - \frac{dy}{x+y},
</me>

<p>
where <m>C</m> is the boundary of the square with vertices
<m>(1,0)</m>, <m>(2,1)</m>, <m>(1,2)</m>, and <m>(0,1)</m>, shown in
<xref ref="fig-green-diamond"/>. Make suitable substitutions in your
integral to make the limits of integration numbers as opposed to
functions.
</p>
</statement>
</example>

<solution xml:id="solution-green-diamond">
<p>
In this example we have
</p>

<me>
M(x,y) = \frac{1}{x+y}, \qquad N(x,y) = \frac{-1}{x+y},
</me>

<p>
and thus
</p>

<me>
\frac{\partial N}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial M}{\partial y}
= \frac{1}{(x+y)^2} - \frac{-1}{(x+y)^2}
= \frac{2}{(x+y)^2}.
</me>

<p>
Then we can use the tangential form of Green's theorem,
<xref ref="eq-green-tangential"/>, to evaluate the line integral:
</p>

<me>
\oint_C M\, dx + N\, dy
= \iint_R \left( \frac{\partial N}{\partial x}
- \frac{\partial M}{\partial y} \right) dx\, dy
= \iint_R \frac{2}{(x+y)^2}\, dx\, dy.
</me>

<p>
The region <m>R</m> is the square bounded by the four lines
<m>y = x + 1</m>, <m>y = x - 1</m>, <m>y = -x + 1</m>, and
<m>y = -x + 3</m>, so the natural substitution uses the boundary
expressions:
</p>

<me>
u = x + y, \qquad v = y - x,
</me>

<p>
under which the region becomes the rectangle
<m>1 \le u \le 3</m>, <m>-1 \le v \le 1</m>. Solving for <m>x</m> and
<m>y</m> gives <m>x = \tfrac{u-v}{2}</m>, <m>y = \tfrac{u+v}{2}</m>,
so the Jacobian is
</p>

<me>
\frac{\partial(x,y)}{\partial(u,v)}
= \begin{vmatrix}
\tfrac{1}{2} \amp -\tfrac{1}{2} \\
\tfrac{1}{2} \amp \tfrac{1}{2}
\end{vmatrix}
= \frac{1}{2}.
</me>

<p>
Therefore
</p>

<md>
<mrow>\iint_R \frac{2}{(x+y)^2}\, dx\, dy
\amp= \int_{-1}^{1} \int_1^3 \frac{2}{u^2} \cdot \frac{1}{2}\,
du\, dv
= \int_{-1}^{1} \left[ -\frac{1}{u} \right]_1^3 dv</mrow>
<mrow>\amp= \int_{-1}^{1} \frac{2}{3}\, dv = \frac{4}{3}.</mrow>
</md>

<p>
Green's theorem converted the line integral into a double integral,
and the substitution<mdash/>exactly the technique of the previous
section<mdash/>made its limits constant. See
<xref ref="fig-green-diamond-video"/> for the animation.
</p>
</solution>

<figure xml:id="fig-green-diamond-video">
<caption>
Green's theorem turns the line integral around the tilted square into
a double integral of <m>2/(x+y)^2</m>, and the substitution
<m>u = x+y</m>, <m>v = y-x</m> straightens the square into a rectangle
with constant limits.
</caption>
<video xml:id="video-green-diamond"
       source="videos/green_diamond_1080p.mp4"
       width="70%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>

