</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-component-test-alternative">
<title>An Alternative Form of the Component Test for Conservative Fields</title>

<p>
Recall the component test for conservative fields:
</p>

<assemblage xml:id="reminder-component-test">
<title>Reminder: Component Test for Conservative Fields</title>
<p>
Let <m>\mathbf{F} = M(x,y,z)\,\mathbf{i} + N(x,y,z)\,\mathbf{j}
+ P(x,y,z)\,\mathbf{k}</m> be a field on a connected and simply
connected domain whose component functions have continuous first
partial derivatives. Then <m>\mathbf{F}</m> is conservative if and only
if
</p>

<me>
\frac{\partial P}{\partial y} = \frac{\partial N}{\partial z}, \qquad
\frac{\partial M}{\partial z} = \frac{\partial P}{\partial x},
\qquad \text{and} \qquad
\frac{\partial N}{\partial x} = \frac{\partial M}{\partial y}.
</me>
</assemblage>

<p>
The three equalities above are equivalent to
<m>\nabla \times \mathbf{F} = \mathbf{0}</m>, since
</p>

<me>
\nabla \times \mathbf{F} =
\begin{vmatrix}
\mathbf{i} \amp \mathbf{j} \amp \mathbf{k} \\
\dfrac{\partial}{\partial x} \amp \dfrac{\partial}{\partial y} \amp
\dfrac{\partial}{\partial z} \\
M \amp N \amp P
\end{vmatrix}
= \left( \frac{\partial P}{\partial y} - \frac{\partial N}{\partial z}
\right) \mathbf{i}
+ \left( \frac{\partial M}{\partial z} - \frac{\partial P}{\partial x}
\right) \mathbf{j}
+ \left( \frac{\partial N}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial M}{\partial y}
\right) \mathbf{k}.
</me>

<p>
So to check whether a vector field is conservative or not, we can check
whether its curl is zero or not.
</p>

<example xml:id="example-conservative-green">
<title>Conservative Fields and Green's Theorem</title>
<statement>
<p>
Consider the line integral
<m>\displaystyle \oint_C 4x^3 y\, dx + x^4\, dy</m>.
</p>

<p>
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
As you know, the vector form of a line integral is
<m>\displaystyle \oint_C \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{r}</m>. Write down
the vector field <m>\mathbf{F}</m> for the above line integral.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Is this vector field a conservative field? Use the component test to
answer.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Using Green's theorem, confirm your answer to part B.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
</example>

<solution xml:id="solution-conservative-green">
<p>
<em>Part A.</em> The vector field is
</p>

<me>
\mathbf{F}(x,y) = M(x,y)\,\mathbf{i} + N(x,y)\,\mathbf{j}
= 4x^3 y\,\mathbf{i} + x^4\,\mathbf{j}.
</me>

<p>
<em>Part B.</em> Treating <m>\mathbf{F}</m> as a field in space with
<m>P = 0</m>,
</p>

<me>
\nabla \times \mathbf{F} =
\begin{vmatrix}
\mathbf{i} \amp \mathbf{j} \amp \mathbf{k} \\
\dfrac{\partial}{\partial x} \amp \dfrac{\partial}{\partial y} \amp
\dfrac{\partial}{\partial z} \\
M \amp N \amp 0
\end{vmatrix}
= \left( \frac{\partial N}{\partial x}
- \frac{\partial M}{\partial y} \right) \mathbf{k}
= \left( \frac{\partial}{\partial x}(x^4)
- \frac{\partial}{\partial y}(4x^3 y) \right) \mathbf{k}
= \left( 4x^3 - 4x^3 \right)\mathbf{k} = \mathbf{0},
</me>

<p>
and hence the vector field is conservative.
</p>

<p>
<em>Part C.</em> Using the tangential form of Green's theorem,
<xref ref="eq-green-tangential"/>, for any simple closed curve
<m>C</m> enclosing a region <m>D</m>:
</p>

<md>
<mrow>\oint_C 4x^3 y\, dx + x^4\, dy
\amp= \iint_D \left( \frac{\partial N}{\partial x}
- \frac{\partial M}{\partial y} \right) dx\, dy</mrow>
<mrow>\amp= \iint_D \left( \frac{\partial}{\partial x}(x^4)
- \frac{\partial}{\partial y}(4x^3 y) \right) dx\, dy = 0.</mrow>
</md>

<p>
The circulation of a conservative field around <em>every</em> closed
curve vanishes, which confirms part B. The animation in
<xref ref="fig-conservative-video"/> shows the field and several closed
loops, all with zero circulation.
</p>
</solution>

<figure xml:id="fig-conservative-video">
<caption>
The conservative field <m>4x^3 y\,\mathbf{i} + x^4\,\mathbf{j} =
\nabla(x^4 y)</m> has zero curl, so by Green's theorem its circulation
around every closed curve is zero.
</caption>
<video xml:id="video-conservative"
       source="videos/conservative_zero_1080p.mp4"
       width="70%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>

</subsection>

</section>
